I built the Brief A agent, saved it to slot champ-A, and it starts a call. The only live check was one chat turn: the agent gave the right opening and asked "Am I speaking with Ms Lee?". As you asked, I ran no practice cases and no `run_cases.py`, so identity, negotiation and closing have not been tried on a real conversation yet.

- **Structure:** your `code_node` is the start node and leads into opening. From there the call goes to identity, then a silent account lookup, then negotiation, and ends on a hang-up node. The opening has a callback tool, the account lookup carries your `account_post_process_code` unedited, and every stage that can close has `close_call`.
- **Moving between stages:** the call goes to identity only when the person clearly confirms they are the customer. It goes to the account lookup only when the bank reports both security answers verified. It hangs up only after `close_call` succeeds.
- **Failed promise:** if `create_promise` fails twice, the call moves to a small extra node that tells the customer it could not be recorded and closes as `no_promise` / `service_error` (rule 37).
- **Dates and amounts:** every node that handles dates or callbacks shows `{{date_facts}}` and the "never compute a date yourself" instruction. The negotiation tells the model to use each product's own `deadline`, `floor_amount` and `persuasion_attempts` from the account result.
- **Brief vs date facts:** I compared the brief's rules (bands, deadlines, holidays, callback window, "this/next" weekday) with the date facts and found no difference, so nothing was overridden.

The workflow is in `out/champ-A.workflow.json`, and the script that generates it is in `builder_tmp/gen_a.py`.

